(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-08"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Carbon forms more compounds than any other element, from methane to DNA, because its atoms join to each other in chains, rings and networks. Organic compounds are everywhere: in fuels, medicines, plastics, food and living things. This chapter shows how to draw them, group them, name them by IUPAC rules, recognise isomers, and understand how organic reactions happen."},"14.2":{"s":"Organic molecules can be drawn in several ways. A structural formula shows all the bonds. The condensed formula leaves out some or all of the bonds, as in CH₃–CH₃. In the bond line (zig-zag) formula, lines are the C–C bonds and carbon and hydrogen atoms on them are not written; heteroatoms and the hydrogens on them are written (OH, NH₂). Three-dimensional drawings use wedge, Fischer, Newman or sawhorse formulae.","w":["In a bond line formula every corner and end is a carbon atom, with enough hydrogens to make four bonds."]},"14.3":{"s":"Organic compounds are classified by carbon skeleton and by functional group. By skeleton they are open chain (aliphatic) or cyclic. Cyclic compounds are homocyclic (carbocyclic, with only carbon in the ring) or heterocyclic (with another atom such as N, O or S). Homocyclic ones are alicyclic or aromatic. A functional group is the atom or group that decides the chemical behaviour, such as –OH, –CHO or –COOH. A homologous series differs by a CH₂ unit.","w":["Members of a homologous series differ by CH₂ and have similar chemical properties."]},"14.4":{"s":"IUPAC names have three parts: a parent hydrocarbon (the longest chain with the principal functional group), prefixes for branches and other groups, and a suffix for the principal functional group. Number the chain so that the principal functional group gets the lowest locant. Substituents are named as prefixes in alphabetical order, with di-, tri- ignored in the ordering. Many common names such as toluene, aniline and glycerol are still accepted.","w":["Choose the chain that contains the principal functional group first, even if it is not the longest.","Alphabetical order ignores di-, tri- and tetra-."]},"14.5":{"s":"Isomers are compounds with the same molecular formula but different structures. Structural isomers differ in how the atoms are joined: chain isomers (different carbon skeleton), position isomers (functional group at a different place), functional group isomers, metamers (different alkyl groups on each side of a functional group) and tautomers (isomers that change into each other quickly). Stereoisomers have the same bonds but a different arrangement in space.","w":["Same molecular formula is the starting point: C₂H₆O is both ethanol and dimethyl ether (functional group isomers)."]},"14.6":{"s":"In an organic reaction bonds in the substrate break and new bonds form. Homolytic cleavage gives two free radicals and happens with heat or light in non-polar conditions. Heterolytic cleavage gives a cation and an anion and happens in polar solvents. A carbocation has a sextet of electrons; a carbanion has a lone pair. Reagents are electrophiles (electron seeking) or nucleophiles (nucleus seeking). Inductive, resonance and other electronic effects explain stability and reactivity.","w":["Stability of carbocations and free radicals: tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl.","A nucleophile has a lone pair or is negatively charged; an electrophile lacks electrons (a positive ion or a vacant orbital)."]}};
